The default `Thread` is a styled element whose source the CLI copies into your project, by default under `components/assistant-ui/elements/`. You can edit it, and the docs say you can keep `Thread` and swap one message component for your own.

`ThreadPrimitive.Messages`, the primitive for rendering the message list, takes a children render function:

```tsx
<ThreadPrimitive.Messages>
  {({ message }) => {
    if (message.composer.isEditing) return <MyEditComposer />;
    if (message.role === "user") return <MyUserMessage />;
    return <MyAssistantMessage />;
  }}
</ThreadPrimitive.Messages>
```

Its `components` prop is deprecated. The examples build custom messages with `MessagePrimitive.Root` and `MessagePrimitive.Parts`.

So I infer you'd change this in your copied `Thread` file. The docs don't say what that file's code looks like, which lines to change, or whether `Thread` accepts a prop for overriding messages. `add <name> --overwrite` discards your edits, so commit first.

Pages: docs/concepts/components.md, docs/primitives/thread.md

Your custom data should appear in a message as a `data` part, identified by a `name` you choose.

**Register a renderer** with `makeAssistantDataUI({ name, render })`, then render the component it returns. `useAssistantDataUI` also registers a named renderer while your component is mounted, but these pages don't show its arguments.

**Display it** inside `MessagePrimitive.Parts`:

```tsx
{({ part }) => {
  if (part.type === "data") return part.dataRendererUI ?? null;
  return null; // add text and tool cases
}}
```

Returning `null` still auto-renders registered data renderers; `<></>` suppresses them. With `GroupedParts`, return `part.dataRendererUI` for `data`. The styled Thread element already renders data parts.

Older code uses the deprecated `components={{ data: { by_name, Fallback } }}`. Its example components receive `data`, and the fallback receives `name`.

**Backend:** The docs don't say what wire format your stream needs, or how each runtime converts it into `data` parts. Check your runtime's page. They also don't give `render`'s full props.

Pages: docs/primitives/message.md, docs/api-reference/tools/rendering.md, docs/concepts/messages.md

In your message component, use the `MessagePrimitive.Parts` children render function. This recipe combines documented pieces; the pages I read don't show this exact per-name example.

```tsx
<MessagePrimitive.Parts>
  {({ part }) => {
    if (part.type !== "tool-call") return null;
    if (part.toolName === "get_weather") return <WeatherUI {...part} />;
    return part.toolUI ?? null;
  }}
</MessagePrimitive.Parts>
```

Add your text and other part branches before the tool check. `part.toolUI` is the registered UI, or `null` if there isn't one. Returning `null` still lets registered UIs render automatically; `<></>` suppresses them.

You may see `components={{ tools: { by_name: { get_weather: WeatherUI } } }}`, with an optional `tools.Fallback`. The `components` prop is deprecated, although the Tool UI guide still mentions these overrides. With it, globally registered tools take precedence over `by_name`; `tools.Override` handles all calls. v0.15's "tools map" removal names `ToolsState.tools`, and `components` remains deprecated.

Pages: docs/primitives/message.md, docs/tools/tool-ui.md, docs/migrations/v0-15.md

In your `ChatModelAdapter`, declare `run` as an async generator (`async *run`); a plain `async run` can't yield. As chunks arrive, add each to a variable and yield the **full content so far**, because each yield replaces the previous content:

```ts
async *run({ messages, abortSignal }) {
  const stream = await callYourBackend(messages, abortSignal); // your code
  let text = "";
  for await (const delta of stream) {
    text += delta;
    yield { content: [{ type: "text", text }] };
  }
}
```

- Pass `abortSignal` to your `fetch` or SDK call so cancel works.
- With reasoning, add each part only once it has content. Only the last text or reasoning part reports `running`, so an empty trailing text part marks reasoning complete.
- Keep tool calls in a `Map` outside the loop so they don't disappear.

Pass the adapter to `useLocalRuntime`, provide the runtime through `AssistantRuntimeProvider`, and render `<Thread />`. The page doesn't say how to parse a raw `fetch` response stream.

Pages: docs/runtimes/custom/local-runtime.md

In React, subscribe to `thread.runEnd` with `useAuiEvent`, in a component under `AssistantRuntimeProvider`. It can return `null`:

```tsx
useAuiEvent({ scope: "*", event: "thread.runEnd" }, ({ threadId }) => {
  // your code
});
```

With `scope: "*"`, you hear runs from any thread, and `threadId` identifies which one. The plain form, `useAuiEvent("thread.runEnd", handler)`, follows the selected thread where your component sits under the provider. It misses runs that finish after the user switches away. The hook unsubscribes on unmount.

Use events for side effects such as toasts or analytics. To display running status, read `useAuiState((s) => s.thread.isRunning)`. Outside React, `aui.subscribe` fires on every state change, and `aui.thread.getState()` reads the current state. You first have to pass `aui` out from a component under the provider.

The docs don't say whether `runEnd` fires for cancelled or failed runs, or whether it includes the reply. They also don't cover a server-side completion callback.

Pages: docs/guides/state-outside-the-thread.md, docs/concepts/context-api.md
